9. Motion & Interaction Specification
• Standard transition: 150–200 ms ease-out for screen pushes and modal entry; no motion longer than 300 ms
anywhere in the agent app (speed matters more than flourish in the field).
• SOS long-press: a single, short vibration pulse (~150 ms) confirms activation — no repeating buzz pattern.
Page 11MICROFI — UI/UX Design Handoff v2.0 (SAD-aligned)
• Geofence breach: the route line color transition is instantaneous (no animation) since it functions as an alert,
not decoration.
• Loading spinners are discreet (16–20 px, single color) and never block the whole screen except for the two
modal cases defined in Section 8.1.
• Reduced-motion: respect the OS-level "reduce motion" accessibility setting by disabling non-essential
transitions (card entrance animations) while preserving state-change indicators (spinners, badges).
10. Localization & Content Strategy
• Primary languages: French (default, CEMAC francophone zones) and English, selectable at first launch and in
Settings; the Back-Office mirrors both.
• All UI copy lives in externalized string tables (ARB for Flutter, i18n JSON for Next.js) — no hardcoded
strings in the wizard, error dialogs, or receipt template.
• Currency and number formatting always use the space-separated thousands rule (Section 1.2) in both locales;
date/time follow ISO-derived local formats, stored in UTC (TIMESTAMPTZ) and rendered in branch-local
time (branch.timezone).
• Text expansion budget: French strings run ~15–20% longer than English; buttons and banners must
accommodate this without truncation, especially the 48 dp action buttons.
• Low-literacy support: icons and color coding (Section 1.1, 3) are treated as a first-class communication
channel alongside text, not a decoration, consistent with the SAD's low-literacy field-user constraint.
